Data provenance
Each file below holds tables a generator under tools/gen generates, and its
header names their source and that source's licence ("Generated by
tools/gen/<generator> from <source> (<licence>): ..."), as the paragraph
about it here does; scripts/third_party_licenses_check.py ports checks that
the two agree.

crates/unityrs-core/src/typetree/commonstring.rs: the 108 common strings
of UnityPy 1.25.2 (MIT), from UnityPy/enums/CommonString.py. The table
UnityPy's blob parser reads is its type-tree package's, which unityrs builds
at run time from the package the user supplies; nothing from a package is
committed.

crates/unityrs-core/src/classid.rs: class id names generated from
UnityPy 1.25.2 (MIT), from UnityPy/enums/ClassIDType.py.

crates/unityrs-core/src/tables.rs: the zero of every decimal-digit (Nd) run,
the code points whose lower case differs between Python 3.11 and Rust, and
the two classes that decide a capital sigma's final form (Cased and
Case_Ignorable, as Final_Sigma reads them), generated from the Unicode
Character Database 14.0.0 (Unicode-3.0) as Python 3.11's unicodedata and
str.lower() read it, and the pinned Rust toolchain's char::to_lowercase.

crates/unityrs-dump/src/select/printable.rs: the code points Python 3.11's
str.isprintable() refuses (the general categories Cc, Cf, Cs, Co, Cn, Zl,
Zp and Zs but the space), generated from the Unicode Character Database
14.0.0 (Unicode-3.0) as Python 3.11 reads it.

crates/unityrs-texture/src/astc/tables.rs: the decode-only integer sequence,
colour and weight quantisation tables of Arm astcenc commit 631fb04
(Apache-2.0), generated by tools/gen/astc_tables.py from
astcenc_integer_sequence.cpp, astcenc_quantization.cpp and
astcenc_weight_quant_xfer_tables.cpp. Its notice is under Arm astcenc
above.

The Unicode License v3 text, which covers the Unicode data in both, is
listed under Crates below.

No type-tree package (TPK) file is included in this repository or in any
binary built from it. unityrs-tpk loads one from a path the user supplies.
The TPK tests read
synthetic packages built in code (tools/fixtures/tpk.py) and, in the oracle
image only, UnityPy's own package, from which nothing is committed.

No game data is included in this repository. Test inputs are synthetic and
built in code.

